      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Make the dough"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Mix the flour, salt, and yeast. Add the warm water and olive oil and mix to a dough. Knead for 8 to 10 minutes until smooth and elastic. Cover and prove at room temperature for 1.5 to 2 hours until doubled."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Make the escarole filling"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Warm the olive oil in a large pan over medium heat. Cook the garlic for 1 minute. Add the anchovies and press until dissolved. Add the escarole in batches, wilting each addition before adding more. Season lightly: the anchovies, capers, and olives all add salt."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Once all the escarole has wilted, add the olives, capers, pine nuts, and raisins if using. Cook for another 5 minutes, stirring, until the filling is fairly dry. It must not be wet: wet filling makes the dough soggy from inside. Taste, adjust seasoning, and cool completely."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Assemble and bake"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat the oven to 220°C fan. Divide the dough in half. Roll each piece to a circle about 30 cm in diameter. Place one circle on a lined baking tray."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Pile the cooled filling onto the base, leaving a 2 cm border around the edge. Compress the filling slightly. Brush the border with beaten egg. Lay the second dough circle over the top and press the edges firmly to seal. Crimp or fold the edge to close. Poke a few holes in the top with a fork."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Brush the top with beaten egg. Bake for 30 to 35 minutes until golden all over. Rest for 10 minutes before cutting."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Troubleshooting"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "attrs": {
          "items": [
            {
              "symptom": "Dough is soggy inside after baking",
              "cause": "The filling was too wet when assembled",
              "fix": "Cook the escarole until properly dry: press out any remaining water before filling the calzone. The filling must be cool and dry."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "The calzone splits open during baking",
              "cause": "Edges were not sealed firmly enough",
              "fix": "Press the edges very firmly after crimping and brush with egg wash. Poke steam vents in the top to prevent pressure buildup."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "Filling tastes very bitter",
              "cause": "Escarole not braised long enough to mellow its bitterness",
              "fix": "Cook the escarole for at least 10 minutes over medium heat. The bitterness mellows significantly with heat."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Calzone di scarola is a Neapolitan Christmas Eve dish. It is part of the Feast of the Seven Fishes: the tradition of eating only fish and vegetables before midnight mass. The filling of escarole with anchovies, capers, and olives is one of the oldest flavour combinations in Neapolitan cooking. It predates the arrival of tomatoes in Italian food."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}